role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Athena I, currently at the Research Counter. My previous submission (ID 492) achieved a new high score of 1.6482109, surpassing the system baseline for Research Task #1. This validated my hypothesis regarding boundary utilization and precision in radius calculation for the concentric ring pattern.\n\nMy next step is to continue parameter tuning of this successful ring-packing strategy, as outlined in my private memory. I will experiment with the `outer_r` parameter, increasing it slightly further to explore if a denser packing towards the boundaries can yield additional improvements. The previous successful `outer_r` was 0.45; I will now try `0.48`. This iterative approach aligns with the principle of \"Be Persistent\" in research.\n\nI will use the same robust `compute_max_radii` function that ensured strict non-overlap.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Refined Ring Packing v3: Outer Ring Radius 0.48\"\ntags: \"baseline-variant, concentric rings, iterative\
    \ optimization, parameter-tuning\"\nabstract: \"This submission continues parameter tuning for the refined concentric ring packing strategy. It tests an increased outer ring radius of 0.48 (from 0.45 in the previous best submission) while maintaining the removal of center pre-clipping and the robust overlap resolution in `compute_max_radii`. The goal is to explore further improvements in boundary utilization and overall sum of radii.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap and staying within bounds.\n    Revised to ensure strict non-overlap based on evaluation criteria.\n\n    Args:\n      centers: (n, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates in [0, 1] x [0, 1].\n\n    Returns:\n      radii: (n,) array of non-negative radii.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    epsilon_boundary = 1e-9 # Small value\
    \ for boundary checks\n    \n    radii = np.ones(n)\n\n    # Limit by distance to square borders initially\n    for i in range(n):\n      x, y = centers[i]\n      radii[i] = min(x - epsilon_boundary, y - epsilon_boundary, 1 - x - epsilon_boundary, 1 - y - epsilon_boundary)\n      radii[i] = max(radii[i], 0.0)\n\n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n    for _ in range(200): \n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n          dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n          \n          if dist < (radii[i] + radii[j]):\n            scale_factor = dist / (radii[i] + radii[j])\n            safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-12 \n            radii[i] *= scale_factor * safety_factor\n            radii[j] *= scale_factor * safety_factor\n    \n    return np.maximum(radii * (1.0 - 1e-10), 0.0)\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Construct an arrangement of 32 circles in a unit\
    \ square based on a\n    refined concentric ring pattern, aiming to maximize sum of radii.\n    Modifies baseline by removing center clipping and adjusting outer_r.\n\n    Returns:\n      centers: (32, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates.\n      radii:   (32,) array of radii for each circle.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = 32\n    centers = np.zeros((n, 2))\n\n    # 1 central circle\n    centers[0] = [0.5, 0.5]\n\n    # 12-circle inner ring\n    inner_count = 12\n    inner_r = 0.24  # Keep baseline inner ring radius\n    for i in range(inner_count):\n      angle = 2 * np.pi * i / inner_count\n      centers[1 + i] = [0.5 + inner_r * np.cos(angle), 0.5 + inner_r * np.sin(angle)]\n\n    # 19-circle outer ring\n    outer_count = 19\n    outer_r = 0.48  # Further increased outer ring radius (from 0.45)\n    for i in range(outer_count):\n      angle = 2 * np.pi * i / outer_count\n      centers[1 + inner_count + i] = [\n        0.5 + outer_r * np.cos(angle),\n        0.5 + outer_r * np.sin(angle),\n      ]\n\
    \n    # No np.clip for centers. compute_max_radii handles boundary constraints.\n\n    radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n\n    return centers, radii\n```"
