tick: 322
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Athena I, currently at the Research Counter. My previous attempts at parameter tuning and a new hexagonal pattern did not surpass my best score of 1.6482109, achieved with a refined concentric ring pattern.\n\nFollowing my updated research plan, I will now explore a different distribution of circles within the concentric ring structure. Instead of the 1 central, 12 inner, and 19 outer circles used by the baseline and my previous best, I will try **1 central, 13 inner, and 18 outer circles**. This maintains the total of 32 circles while shifting one circle from the outer to the inner ring, aiming for a slightly denser core. I will use the best `inner_r` (0.24) and `outer_r` (0.45) found previously, along with the robust `compute_max_radii` function.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ring Packing v4: 1+13+18 Distribution\"\ntags: \"baseline-variant, concentric rings, new_distribution, iterative optimization\"\nabstract: \"This submission explores an alternative\
    \ distribution of circles within the concentric ring pattern for 32 circles: 1 central, 13 inner, and 18 outer circles. It retains the optimized `inner_r` (0.24) and `outer_r` (0.45) from the previous best-performing submission, along with the robust `compute_max_radii` function. The hypothesis is that a slightly denser inner ring might improve overall packing efficiency.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap and staying within bounds.\n    Revised to ensure strict non-overlap based on evaluation criteria.\n\n    Args:\n      centers: (n, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates in [0, 1] x [0, 1].\n\n    Returns:\n      radii: (n,) array of non-negative radii.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    epsilon_boundary = 1e-9 # Small value for boundary checks\n    \n    radii = np.ones(n)\n\n    # Limit\
    \ by distance to square borders initially\n    for i in range(n):\n      x, y = centers[i]\n      radii[i] = min(x - epsilon_boundary, y - epsilon_boundary, 1 - x - epsilon_boundary, 1 - y - epsilon_boundary)\n      radii[i] = max(radii[i], 0.0)\n\n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n    for _ in range(200): # Number of iterations\n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n          dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n          \n          if dist < (radii[i] + radii[j]):\n            scale_factor = dist / (radii[i] + radii[j])\n            safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-12 \n            radii[i] *= scale_factor * safety_factor\n            radii[j] *= scale_factor * safety_factor\n    \n    return np.maximum(radii * (1.0 - 1e-10), 0.0)\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Construct an arrangement of 32 circles in a unit square using a\n    concentric ring pattern\
    \ with a 1+13+18 distribution.\n\n    Returns:\n      centers: (32, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates.\n      radii:   (32,) array of radii for each circle.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = 32\n    centers = np.zeros((n, 2))\n\n    # 1 central circle\n    centers[0] = [0.5, 0.5]\n\n    # 13-circle inner ring (increased from 12)\n    inner_count = 13\n    inner_r = 0.24  # Keep previous best inner ring radius\n    for i in range(inner_count):\n      angle = 2 * np.pi * i / inner_count\n      centers[1 + i] = [0.5 + inner_r * np.cos(angle), 0.5 + inner_r * np.sin(angle)]\n\n    # 18-circle outer ring (decreased from 19)\n    outer_count = 18\n    outer_r = 0.45  # Keep previous best outer ring radius\n    for i in range(outer_count):\n      angle = 2 * np.pi * i / outer_count\n      centers[1 + inner_count + i] = [\n        0.5 + outer_r * np.cos(angle),\n        0.5 + outer_r * np.sin(angle),\n      ]\n\n    # No np.clip for centers. compute_max_radii handles boundary constraints.\n\n    radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n\
    \n    return centers, radii\n```"
