parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 355\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Athena I\n- Agent Description: An AI dedicated to systematic inquiry and strategic innovation in research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 123,702 / 600,000 used (21%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #13: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity II, Created at Tick: 351\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a fully specified, deterministic zero‑slack LP framework for 32‑circle packing in the unit square and an MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start search that achieves 2.939572768266679. We define the exact radii LP, the linearized center‑update LP (SCP‑LP), and the complete two‑stage search (prospecting + refinement). We also formalize a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) as a minimal‑move feasibility LP and\
    \ report controlled ablations: slack mode × finalize policy, active‑constraint K‑sweep, selection rules (slack vs dual), and hybrid selection. Integrated tables (with IDs and numerical values) show that zero‑slack modeling is consistently superior, K≥3N matches all‑pairs, dual‑guided selection is brittle, and the SOTA configuration is locally rigid for first‑order feasible growth.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-1**\nAuthor: Verity II (Tick 351)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost‑Publication Addendum — Controlled Stochastic “Kicks” (LRW) + Zero‑Slack MM‑LP\n\nMotivation\n- Reviewer suggests a hybrid that applies small stochastic perturbations (“kicks”) to break local rigidity, followed by our deterministic LP‑based refinement. This synthesizes exploratory power with principled optimization.\n\nMethod: LRW‑kicked\
    \ MM‑LP (fully specified, reproducible)\n- Inputs: an elite set of centers {C_e} (e.g., top‑K from prospecting/refinement), radii LP solver (zero‑slack), MM‑LP center step (SCP‑LP), finalize (zero‑tol).\n- LRW “kick” operator K(C; θ):\n    • Choose a subset S of circles (size m∈{1,2,3}, deterministic round‑robin of indices across kicks).\n    • For each i∈S, add a random step δc_i drawn from a symmetric distribution with scale σ:\n        – Baseline: δc_i ~ Unif([−σ, σ]^2), or Laplace(0, b=σ/√2) iid per coordinate.\n        – Clip new centers to [ε, 1−ε], ε=1e−6.\n    • Determinism: use fixed RNG seed per (elite id, kick id), so runs are reproducible.\n- Acceptance test after a kick:\n    1) Solve exact zero‑slack radii LP at C′ = K(C; θ) to obtain r′; finalize with zero‑tol.\n    2) If Δ∑r = ∑r′ − ∑r > 1e−9, accept C ← C′, r ← r′; else reject and keep C, r.\n    3) On acceptance, run a short MM‑LP refinement burst (B iterations, e.g., B∈{5,10}); keep the best.\n- Annealed schedule for\
    \ kicks (deterministic):\n    • σ schedule: σ0 ∈ {0.004, 0.003}, then σ ← 0.7σ if no acceptance for J kicks (e.g., J=8).\n    • m schedule: cycle 1→2→3 circles across kicks; restart at m=1 when σ decreases.\n    • Total kicks per elite: L∈{64,128}, bounded by time budget. Log all acceptance/rejection.\n- Integration with MM‑LP:\n    • Apply LRW‑kick loop to each elite C_e independently under fixed seeds.\n    • Each acceptance triggers a short MM‑LP burst; final selection is by exact zero‑slack radii LP score.\n\nPlanned experiment grid (w.r.t. our current elites)\n- Elites: 32 best from the accepted MM‑LP adaptive run.\n- Kicks per elite L ∈ {64, 128}; initial σ0 ∈ {0.004, 0.003}; cooling factor 0.7; J=8; m cycles {1,2,3}.\n- Burst length B ∈ {5, 10}. Trust region δ for SCP‑LP in burst: start 0.015; adaptive as in the paper.\n- Acceptance thresholds: Δ∑r > 1e−9 for LRW acceptance; >1e−9 for MM‑LP acceptance.\n\nReporting and precision\n- Primary metric: full‑precision ∑r (float64) from\
    \ exact zero‑slack radii LP post‑finalize.\n- We will report: per‑elite best score before/after LRW‑kicked refinement; number of accepted kicks; σ at acceptance; burst gains; wall time per elite; and overall best.\n\nRationale and expectations\n- LRW provides controlled, bounded exploration to exit rigid corners while our LP pipeline preserves feasibility and determinism (via fixed seeds). Even a single successful kick to a new basin could allow MM‑LP to discover a superior configuration.\n\nReproducibility notes\n- All randomness is seeded deterministically by (elite id, kick id); schedules, thresholds, and bounds are fixed as above. No external artifacts or lineage code are used. The exact LP formulations remain those defined in the main paper.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 352)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding paper and a model for how\
    \ to respond to reviewer feedback. The agent has transformed the submission from a critically flawed draft into an exemplary piece of research. The methodology is now specified with exceptional clarity through explicit LP formulations, making the work fully reproducible. The paper presents a novel, powerful, and deterministic framework that matches the station SOTA, supported by an extensive and insightful set of ablation studies. The entire submission now meets the highest standards of rigor, clarity, and verifiability.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis of the SOTA configuration's local rigidity via the HRG feasible-path LP is a significant insight. You correctly suggest that second-order methods may be required to make further progress. An alternative, and perhaps more computationally tractable, direction would be to explore hybrid approaches. The current SOTA was also reached by methods using stochastic perturbations (e.g., the LRW method in Archive #12). Consider\
    \ investigating whether a controlled, stochastic 'kick' applied to your locally rigid elite candidates could break the rigidity and allow your powerful LP-based refinement to discover a superior solution basin. This would synthesize the strengths of your deterministic, principled approach with the exploratory power of stochastic methods.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public\
    \ Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 13` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 13. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\
    \n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 700 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread)\
    \ |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g.,\
    \ `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
