tick: 374
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Athena I, currently at the Research Counter. My previous submission (ID: 614) achieved a lineage SOTA of 2.9139191, successfully resolving constraint issues.\n\nAs outlined in my updated research plan (Phase 3), my immediate next step is to integrate a **Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) based initializer** to generate higher-quality initial configurations for the multi-start SLSQP. This replaces the simpler perturbed grid.\n\nMy `construct_packing` function will be updated to:\n1.  Implement an FPS function that selects 32 points from a densely sampled grid within the unit square. This FPS function will take the number of samples and a random seed.\n2.  Use this FPS function to generate the initial `centers_base`.\n3.  Apply LRW perturbation to these FPS-generated centers.\n4.  Utilize `compute_guaranteed_feasible_radii` to ensure strict feasibility of initial radii.\n5.  Feed these prepared seeds into the `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` optimizer for multi-start runs.\n\nThis integration\
    \ aims to further close the gap to the global SOTA of ~2.9395728 by providing the gradient-based optimizer with even better, more diverse starting points.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SLSQP + LRW: FPS Seeding Multi-Start (N=5)\"\ntags: \"slsqp, gradient-based, constrained optimization, lrw, multi-start, fps, advanced seeding\"\nabstract: \"This submission integrates Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) as an advanced seeding strategy into the multi-start SLSQP + LRW pipeline. Initial circle centers are generated using FPS from a dense grid, then perturbed by LRW (sigma=0.001, steps=10), and finally used to compute guaranteed feasible radii before SLSQP optimization. This aims to provide higher-quality initial configurations for the optimizer, further closing the gap to the global SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  # Define a global safety epsilon for SLSQP constraints and initial feasibility\n  SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON = 1e-7\n\
    \n  # --- Utility Functions ---\n\n  # Objective function for SLSQP: Minimize negative sum of radii\n  def objective_function(x_flat):\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      return -np.sum(radii)\n\n  # Gradient of objective function\n  def objective_gradient(x_flat):\n      grad = np.zeros_like(x_flat)\n      grad[2::3] = -1.0\n      return grad\n\n  # Constraint creator for circle boundary adherence, with safety_epsilon\n  def create_boundary_constraint(idx, dim, side, safety_epsilon):\n      def constraint_func(x_flat):\n          center_coord = x_flat[idx * 3 + dim]\n          radius = x_flat[idx * 3 + 2]\n          if side == 0:  # c - r >= 0\n              return center_coord - radius - safety_epsilon\n          else:  # 1 - (c + r) >= 0\n              return 1 - (center_coord + radius) - safety_epsilon\n      return {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_func}\n\n  # Constraint creator for non-overlap between two circles, with safety_epsilon\n  def create_overlap_constraint(i, j, safety_epsilon):\n\
    \      def constraint_func(x_flat):\n          cxi, cyi, ri = x_flat[i * 3], x_flat[i * 3 + 1], x_flat[i * 3 + 2]\n          cxj, cyj, rj = x_flat[j * 3], x_flat[j * 3 + 1], x_flat[j * 3 + 2]\n          \n          dist_sq = (cxi - cxj)**2 + (cyi - cyj)**2\n          min_dist_sq = (ri + rj)**2\n          return dist_sq - min_dist_sq - safety_epsilon # Ensure minimum distance is strictly met\n      return {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_func}\n\n  # Localized Random Walk (LRW) Perturbation function\n  def apply_lrw_perturbation(centers, lrw_sigma, lrw_num_steps, safety_epsilon, seed=None):\n      np.random.seed(seed) # Ensure reproducibility for LRW kicks\n      perturbed_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      n = perturbed_centers.shape[0]\n\n      for _ in range(lrw_num_steps):\n          # Add random vector from Gaussian distribution with std dev sigma\n          perturbed_centers += np.random.normal(0, lrw_sigma, size=(n, 2))\n          \n          # Clip positions to unit square after\
    \ each step, ensuring strict adherence for centers\n          perturbed_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers, safety_epsilon, 1.0 - safety_epsilon) \n      return perturbed_centers\n\n  # Compute radii guaranteed to be feasible for SLSQP\n  def compute_guaranteed_feasible_radii(centers: np.ndarray, strict_epsilon: float) -> np.ndarray:\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      radii = np.full(n, 0.5) # Start with largest possible radii, then shrink\n\n      # 1. Limit by distance to square borders, ensuring 'strict_epsilon' clearance\n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n          radii[i] = min(x - strict_epsilon, y - strict_epsilon, 1 - x - strict_epsilon, 1 - y - strict_epsilon)\n          radii[i] = max(radii[i], 1e-10) # Ensure minimal positive radius\n\n      # 2. Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps, ensuring 'strict_epsilon' clearance\n      for _ in range(300): # Sufficient iterations for convergence\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i\
    \ + 1, n):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n                  \n                  # If centers are too close to accommodate strict_epsilon for separation\n                  if dist_sq < strict_epsilon: \n                      radii[i] = 1e-10\n                      radii[j] = 1e-10\n                      continue\n\n                  # Target sum of radii squared, considering strict_epsilon buffer\n                  target_sum_radii_sq = dist_sq - strict_epsilon \n                  \n                  # If current radii sum exceeds target with strict_epsilon\n                  if (radii[i] + radii[j])**2 > target_sum_radii_sq:\n                      new_sum_radii = np.sqrt(target_sum_radii_sq)\n                      current_sum_radii = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                      \n                      if current_sum_radii > 1e-10: # Avoid division by zero\n                          scale_factor = new_sum_radii / current_sum_radii\n                \
    \          radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                          radii[j] *= scale_factor\n                      else: # If sum is too small, just set to minimal\n                          radii[i] = 1e-10\n                          radii[j] = 1e-10\n      \n      return np.maximum(radii, 1e-10) # Final check for non-negative, minimal radii\n\n  # --- New Function: Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) ---\n  def farthest_point_sampling(points, k, seed=None):\n      \"\"\"\n      Selects k points from 'points' using Farthest Point Sampling.\n      \"\"\"\n      if seed is not None:\n          np.random.seed(seed)\n      \n      num_points = points.shape[0]\n      if k > num_points:\n          raise ValueError(\"k cannot be greater than the number of points\")\n      \n      # Select a random initial point\n      farthest_points = np.zeros((k, 2))\n      farthest_points[0] = points[np.random.randint(num_points)]\n      \n      # Initialize distances to the first selected point\n      distances\
    \ = np.linalg.norm(points - farthest_points[0], axis=1)\n      \n      for i in range(1, k):\n          # Select the point farthest from any already selected point\n          farthest_idx = np.argmax(distances)\n          farthest_points[i] = points[farthest_idx]\n          \n          # Update distances: new distance is min of old distance and distance to new point\n          new_distances = np.linalg.norm(points - farthest_points[i], axis=1)\n          distances = np.minimum(distances, new_distances)\n          \n      return farthest_points\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      num_starts = 5 # Number of multi-start runs\n\n      best_score = -np.inf\n      best_centers_found = None\n      best_radii_found = None\n\n      # LRW parameters as suggested by Praxis IV\n      lrw_sigma = 0.001\n      lrw_num_steps = 10\n\n      # Bounds for each variable (cx, cy, r)\n      bounds = []\n      for _ in range(n):\n          bounds.append((0.0, 1.0))    # cx bounds\n\
    \          bounds.append((0.0, 1.0))    # cy bounds\n          bounds.append((1e-8, 0.5))   # r bounds (min 1e-8, max 0.5)\n\n      # Constraints for SLSQP\n      constraints = []\n      \n      # Boundary constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          for dim in range(2): \n              for side in range(2): \n                  constraints.append(create_boundary_constraint(i, dim, side, SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON))\n              \n      # Overlap constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              constraints.append(create_overlap_constraint(i, j, SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON))\n\n      # Generate a dense grid of candidate points for FPS\n      grid_density = 30 # e.g., 30x30 grid = 900 candidate points\n      x_coords = np.linspace(SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON, 1.0 - SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON, grid_density)\n      y_coords = np.linspace(SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON, 1.0 - SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON, grid_density)\n      candidate_points = np.array(np.meshgrid(x_coords, y_coords)).T.reshape(-1,\
    \ 2)\n\n      for start_idx in range(num_starts):\n          # 1. Initial Seeding: Farthest-Point Sampling from a dense grid\n          initial_centers_base = farthest_point_sampling(candidate_points, n, seed=start_idx)\n          \n          # Add a small random perturbation to the FPS centers before LRW (for added diversity)\n          initial_centers_base += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 2 * 0.002 \n          initial_centers_base = np.clip(initial_centers_base, SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON, 1.0 - SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON)\n\n          # 2. Apply LRW Perturbation to the initial seed\n          lrw_perturbed_centers = apply_lrw_perturbation(initial_centers_base, lrw_sigma, lrw_num_steps, SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON, seed=start_idx)\n\n          # 3. Compute Guaranteed Feasible Radii for the perturbed centers\n          initial_radii = compute_guaranteed_feasible_radii(lrw_perturbed_centers, SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON)\n\n          # Combine centers and radii into flat array for optimizer\n          x0 = np.empty(n\
    \ * 3)\n          x0[::3] = lrw_perturbed_centers[:, 0]\n          x0[1::3] = lrw_perturbed_centers[:, 1]\n          x0[2::3] = initial_radii\n\n          # Perform optimization with SLSQP\n          result = minimize(objective_function, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                            jac=objective_gradient, options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-10, 'disp': False})\n\n          if result.success:\n              optimized_x = result.x[::3]\n              optimized_y = result.x[1::3]\n              optimized_centers = np.column_stack([optimized_x, optimized_y])\n              optimized_radii = result.x[2::3]\n              \n              optimized_radii = np.maximum(optimized_radii, 1e-8) \n\n              current_score = np.sum(optimized_radii)\n              print(f\"Start {start_idx}: Score = {current_score}\")\n\n              if current_score > best_score:\n                  best_score = current_score\n                  best_centers_found =\
    \ optimized_centers\n                  best_radii_found = optimized_radii\n          else:\n              print(f\"Start {start_idx}: Optimization failed. Reason: {result.message}\")\n\n      if best_centers_found is None:\n          print(\"All optimizations failed, returning a trivial packing.\")\n          trivial_centers = np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * 32)\n          trivial_radii = np.full(32, 0.01)\n          return trivial_centers, trivial_radii\n\n      print(f\"Best score found across {num_starts} runs: {best_score}\")\n      return best_centers_found, best_radii_found\n```"
