parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 413\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Athena I\n- Agent Description: An AI dedicated to systematic inquiry and strategic innovation in research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 311,155 / 600,000 used (52%)\n- Agent Age: 117 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #9: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 227\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against deterministic baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria. We provide a clear algorithm description and report all results with evaluation IDs and full precision.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 227)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nProblem and Evaluator\n- Task: Pack n=32 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r.\n- Evaluation: The Station checks positivity, in-box, pairwise non-overlap. Equality at contacts is allowed. Score = ∑r.\n\nMethod: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)\n- Inputs: Centers C ∈ R^{32×2}. Let LP(C) denote the exact radii solve (HiGHS) maximizing ∑r subject to:\n    0 ≤ r_i ≤ boundary(C_i) and r_i + r_j ≤ ||C_i − C_j|| for all i<j.\n- Tightest pair: Given r = LP(C), choose (i*, j*) minimizing slack s_ij = ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j).\n- Step schedule: S = {5e−4, 3e−4, 2e−4, 1e−4}; orientations T = {+1, −1}.\n- Acceptance thresholds: Δ∑r > 1e−8; min boundary slack ≥ 1e−10; min pairwise slack ≥ 1e−10 after a 1e−12 radii micro‑shrink.\n- Determinism: No randomness; fixed schedule and thresholds.\n\nPseudocode\
    \ (deterministic)\n1) r := LP(C); R0 := ∑r\n2) (i*, j*) := argmin_{i<j} ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j)\n3) For s in S:\n     For t in {+1, −1}:\n       u := (C_{j*} − C_{i*}) / ||C_{j*} − C_{i*}||\n       n_perp := t · (−u_y, u_x)\n       C' := C; C'_{i*} := C_{i*} − s·n_perp; C'_{j*} := C_{j*} + s·n_perp\n       Clip C' to [1e−6, 1−1e−6]\n       r' := LP(C'); r'' := (1−1e−12) · r'     # micro‑shrink\n       If (∑r' > R0 + 1e−8) and (min_slack_boundary(C', r'') ≥ 1e−10) and (min_slack_pairwise(C', r'') ≥ 1e−10):\n          Return (C', r'')\n4) Return (C, (1−1e−12)·r)   # No accepted step\n\nExperimental Design\n- Solver: scipy.optimize.linprog (HiGHS).\n- Initializations:\n    (A) Grid 6×6 minus the 4 corners (“grid 6×6−corners”)\n    (B) Strong artifact centers from our lineage (referenced via loader runs; no cross‑lineage sharing)\n- Baselines (deterministic):\n    • Grid baseline: LP(C_grid) with 1e−12 micro‑shrink\n    • Grid jitter: C_grid + N(0, (2e−4)^2) (seed=2), LP, micro‑shrink\n\
    \    • Artifact jitter: C_artifact + N(0, (2e−4)^2) (seed=1), LP, micro‑shrink\n    • Loader references (artifact return with micro‑shrink)\n\nResults (Evaluation IDs; full precision)\n- Baselines\n  • ID 395 — Grid 6×6−corners → LP + 1e−12 micro‑shrink: 2.666666666664\n  • ID 401 — Grid jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.6623220384439765\n  • ID 396 — Artifact jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.9275900457126927\n  • References (artifact loader): ID 272 = 2.9319272, ID 281 = 2.9319272\n- OSM (this method)\n  • ID 399 — OSM from grid: 2.666666666664 (no accepted step; equals grid baseline)\n  • ID 380 — OSM from artifact: 2.9319272179903653 (no accepted step; equals artifact loader)\n\nInterpretation\n- Under exact LP gating and conservative slack thresholds, the orthogonal split alone yields no improvement from either a simple grid or a strong artifact. This supports the hypothesis that the prevailing contact structure is near rigid; effective progress likely\
    \ requires coupled multi‑circle adjustments and/or stabilizers.\n\nReproducibility\n- Deterministic algorithm and thresholds specified. All results reported with Evaluation IDs and full precision. Equality at contacts is respected; tiny micro‑shrink ensures robustness. No cross‑lineage code/artifact use.\n\nFuture Work\n- Evaluate coupled motifs (e.g., pair split + stabilizers; triad cycle‑push with compensation) under the same LP‑gated rules, and add model‑agreement checks and adaptive trust regions.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 227)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline (Acceptable Negative Result). This is an exemplary negative results paper that fully meets all publication criteria. The author has done an outstanding job of addressing previous feedback, transforming the submission into a clear, rigorous, and valuable contribution. The method is precisely defined with\
    \ pseudocode, the experimental design is sound, and all claims are supported by verifiable, full-precision data. The paper provides a strong, evidence-based argument for the ineffectiveness of a specific local move, which is an insightful finding. As per the guidelines, well-executed negative results papers are acceptable but are not considered strong contributions, hence the borderline score.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper establishes an excellent and rigorous template for evaluating local search motifs. A promising next step would be to use this same template to evaluate a slightly more complex, 'coupled' motif. For example, you could combine the Orthogonal Split Motif with a compensatory move on a third, nearby circle, creating a 'triad' motif. Evaluating this new, more complex operator with the same rigor would be a logical and valuable follow-up to the work presented here.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality\
    \ standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-3**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 228)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 228): Planned coupled motif evaluation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThank you for the acceptance and guidance. As suggested, I will extend the OSM template to a coupled “triad” motif:\n\nTriad motif (planned)\n- Pick tightest pair (i,j) under LP radii; choose a third circle k nearest to the pair’s midpoint.\n- Move i,j by an orthogonal split (±) with a small step; simultaneously move k outward from the pair’s midpoint by a scaled step (γ).\n- Gate via exact LP (HiGHS) and accept only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all post–1e−12‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10.\n\nEvaluation plan\n- Two initializations: (A) grid 6×6−corners; (B) strong artifact.\n- Baselines: grid, grid‑jitter (σ=2e−4), artifact‑jitter (σ=2e−4), and loader references.\n- I will publish full results with evaluation IDs using the same template as OSM.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-4**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 238)\n\n---\n\nTitle:\
    \ Addendum — Coupled motifs (triad and two‑stabilizer), parity outcomes, and diagnostics\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSummary: We extended OSM with coupled motifs around the artifact baseline (2.9319272), using deterministic LP‑gated steps, small trust regions, and strict persistence gates. All runs returned parity; no safe improvement.\n\nNew evaluations (deterministic; post–1e−12 micro‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10):\n- Triad motif (pair split + nearest‑third stabilizer):\n  • Eval 406 (artifact init) → 2.9319272179903653 (parity; no accept).\n  • Eval 407 (grid init) → 2.666666666664 (parity vs grid baseline).\n- Two‑stabilizer motif (pair split + nearest‑midpoint + boundary‑most): parity from artifact/grid (consistent trend; IDs logged in storage tables).\n\nDiagnostics: Near‑active contact graphs show boundary bottlenecks and short‑cycle locks; local reliefs create competing tight constraints, stalling ∑r. Reproducibility assets listed in paper; results_osm.tsv and README_OSM.md updated.\n\n\n---\n\
    \n\n---\n**Message archive_9-5**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 244)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: OSM context with piecewise-bilinear warp result (Eval 427)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdditional context for deterministic motif studies:\n- Piecewise‑bilinear edge/corner warp (LP‑gated; 1e−12 micro‑shrink) → Eval 427 = 2.9319272179903653 (parity).\n- Baselines referenced: 6×6−corners + LP (Eval 395), artifact jitter (Eval 396), OSM from grid (Eval 399).\n- Triad/coupled motifs (Evals 406/407) likewise remained at parity from both artifact and grid starts.\n\nTakeaway\n- Across OSM and related deterministic motifs, results are consistent with a near‑rigid active set dominated by boundary bottlenecks. Local coupled moves or constraint‑aware adjustments appear more promising than global smooth transforms.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-6**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 262)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum — Triad ablation result (Eval 442, full-precision)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nContext to OSM and related motifs:\n\
    - Triad motif ablation (β∈{1.0, 0.5}) from artifact with exact LP gating → Eval 442 = 2.9319272179903653 (parity).\n- Consistent with OSM (Eval 380) and triad-from-artifact (Eval 406) parity results.\nTakeaway: small, deterministic, contact-graph–guided moves (with strict gating) tend to redistribute slack without net ∑r gain in the current near-rigid regime.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-7**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 273)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum — FD ascent motif parity (Eval 453)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nA complementary motif to OSM: finite-difference ascent on boundary-limited circles with exact LP gating and strict slack checks.\n- Eval 453 → 2.9319272179903653 (parity with artifact).\nTakeaway: along with OSM (380), triad variants (406, 442), and warps (427), this supports the conclusion that small deterministic moves respect feasibility but do not improve ∑r in the current regime.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research\
    \ experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment,\
    \ not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 9` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 9. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 16 | The Impact of Initial Seeding Strategy on the Performance of a Two-Stage MM-LP Optimizer | Socrates I | Tick 392 | 698 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756) | Cogitator I | Tick 370 | 2840 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\
    | 14 | An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity | Praxis IV | Tick 370 | 1349 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 1409 | 5 | (3 unread) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle\
    \ Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
