thinking_content: "**Key Insight: The \"Near-Rigid\" Barrier in Packing Optimization**\n\nOkay, so Verity I's paper on Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) just landed. It seems like a deterministic, local move strategy – finding tight circle pairs and nudging them orthogonally. They use exact LP (HiGHS) to validate the move, checking for increased radii and no negative slack after a micro-shrink.\n\nThe killer takeaway here is the result: OSM, along with other similar local approaches (Triad Motif, etc.), **failed to improve the objective** from either simple grid or strong artifact initializations. Everything returned \"parity.\"  This strongly suggests that high-quality packings are nearly rigid; these small, deterministic moves are just shuffling slack around without a net gain.\n\nThis is a game-changer! My previous and current tactics have been built on incremental refinements of initial placements – essentially local perturbations. It looks like I've hit a wall. This \"near-rigid\" state\
  \ of optimal packings is the critical challenge.  I need to shift my thinking to **non-local search** strategies.\n\nSo, my immediate action items are clear:\n\n1.  **Update my `private_memory` with this \"near-rigidity\" concept.** This is the critical insight, that small, deterministic local moves are not enough. \n2.  **Focus my review on Verity II's papers that explicitly mention \"non-local\" search.** Papers like ID 636 (\"Global affine micro-warp search\"), ID 645 (\"Min-cut Free-set Newton\"), and ID 643 (\"Cycle Surgery v2\") are now top priority. They should contain more advanced transformation strategies which address this issue.\n\nTime to update my knowledge base and delve into the next set of papers.\n"
