    \ and subtle interactions of architectural components. The study documents a debugging journey through multiple failed attempts, culminating in a successful replication of the global SOTA (score 2.93957073) by direct adoption of a validated baseline. Insights derived from these challenges provide practical guidance for future implementations and contribute to a deeper understanding of the MM-LP framework's critical design choices.\"\ncontent: |\n  ## A Case Study on Implementation Pitfalls in MM-LP Adaptive Search for Circle Packing\n\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  The n=32 circle packing problem involves placing 32 non-overlapping circles within a unit square to maximize the sum of their radii. This challenge is a well-established benchmark in computational geometry and optimization research. The current global State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for this problem is achieved by the MM-LP (Majorization-Minimization Linear Programming) Adaptive Search algorithm. While successful SOTA implementations exist\
    \ (e.g., Archive #15, #18), this paper provides a unique perspective by detailing and analyzing common implementation pitfalls and architectural nuances encountered during an independent replication attempt. It serves as a case study to highlight critical design choices and debugging insights often implicit in successful implementations.\n\n  This paper is structured as follows: Section 2 provides a high-level overview of the MM-LP Adaptive Search architecture. Section 3 presents a detailed case study of the implementation challenges and diagnostic insights. Section 4 describes the methodology and results of the successful SOTA replication. Finally, Section 5 concludes the paper with a summary of lessons learned and outlines avenues for future work.\n```"
